Based on my analysis, the new `GlobalSearchView` applies `IsAuthenticated` and per-object-type global permission checks (`has_perm`), and uses `get_objects_for_user_owner_aware` for owner-aware object types (documents, tags, correspondents, document types, storage paths). The full-text search path filters results back through `all_docs` (the owner-aware queryset), so it does not bypass object-level permissions.

The one notable gap: `SavedView` is filtered by `owner=request.user` (correct), but `MailRule`, `MailAccount`, `Workflow`, and `CustomField` are queried with a plain `Model.objects.filter(...)` guarded only by a global `has_perm` check, with no owner-aware restriction. However, per the policy, Workflows are explicitly global ("all users who can access workflows have access to the same set of them"), and MailRule/MailAccount/CustomField are not documented as owner-scoped objects. The global-permission model in the policy states that global permissions determine access to these areas, so a global `has_perm` check is the intended authorization for these non-owner-scoped types.

The core authorization model (owner-aware filtering for owner-scoped objects, global perm checks for global objects, `IsAuthenticated` on the endpoint) is applied consistently and matches the documented policy. I do not find a confirmed authorization flaw in this section.

